Reason:
The bug occurs because the `rpmh_flush()` function checks if the controller is dirty (i.e., if there are new requests to process). If it is not dirty, it skips flushing, which means that any previously programmed data in the SLEEP and WAKE TCSes remains uninvalidated. This stale data can trigger unintended behavior when the new data is flushed, leading to incorrect operation of the RPMH system.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
/* Invalidate the TCSes first to avoid stale data */
do {
    ret = rpmh_rsc_invalidate(ctrlr_to_drv(ctrlr));
} while (ret == -EAGAIN);
```
```c
spin_lock_irqsave(&ctrlr->cache_lock, flags);
list_for_each_entry_safe(req, tmp, &ctrlr->batch_cache, list)
    kfree(req);
```
Reason:
The fix involves invalidating the TCSes before flushing new data by calling `rpmh_rsc_invalidate()`. This ensures that any previously programmed data is cleared out, preventing it from interfering with the new data. Additionally, the `invalidate_batch()` function is moved inside `rpmh_invalidate()`, which simplifies the code and ensures that the batch cache is properly invalidated, maintaining the integrity of the RPMH system.

